---
import PageShell from '../../components/nav/PageShell.astro';
import { theorems, deferred } from '../../data/formalization';
import { papers, citedElsewhere } from '../../data/papers';
import { TESTS, numberWord } from '../../data/evidence';
import { LEAN_URL, THEOREM_MAP_URL, LEAN_STATUS_URL, PAPERS_URL, CRATE_URL } from '../../consts';

const title = 'Proof | DeepCausality Quantum';
const description =
  'What is proved in Lean, what tests witness, what is stated and not proved, and which papers the crate implements. Each row names the file that shows it.';

const cap = (s: string) => s.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + s.slice(1);
const split = (s: string) => {
  const [file, item] = s.split(' :: ');
  return { file, item };
};

const cited = papers.filter((p) => p.citedFrom.length > 0);
const uncited = papers.filter((p) => p.citedFrom.length === 0);
---
<PageShell title={title} description={description} path="/proof/">
  <header class="doc-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Proof</p>
    <h1>What is proved, what is tested, and what is neither.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      {cap(numberWord(TESTS.proved))} theorems are proved in Lean, and each has a Rust test that
      witnesses it. The suite runs {TESTS.count} tests, and all of them pass.
      {cap(numberWord(TESTS.deferred))} targets are stated and not proved yet. One tempting claim
      is false, and Lean proves that too.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="theorems">
    <h2>Which theorems are proved?</h2>
    <p>
      The rows come from the quantum section of
      <a href={THEOREM_MAP_URL}><code>lean/THEOREM_MAP.md</code></a>. Lean has no partial trace and
      no Choi–Jamiołkowski layer, so both are built from first principles under
      <a href={LEAN_URL}><code>lean/DeepCausalityFormal/Quantum/</code></a>. The Rust witness is the
      test that checks the same statement on numbers.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="table-stack">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Statement</th><th>Lean declaration</th><th>Rust witness</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {theorems.map((t) => (
            <tr>
              <td data-label="Statement"><span class="num">{t.statement}</span><br /><code class="tid">{t.id}</code></td>
              <td data-label="Lean declaration"><code class="item">{split(t.lean).item}</code><br /><span class="fpath">{split(t.lean).file}</span></td>
              <td data-label="Rust witness"><code class="item">{split(t.witness).item}</code><br /><span class="fpath">{split(t.witness).file}</span></td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="refuted">
    <h2>Which claim turned out false?</h2>
    <p>
      Two commuting operators can lose that property under a partial trace. Take X and Y on a
      system with two parts, with <code>[X, Y] = 0</code>. Lean proves an explicit pair for which
      <code>[Tr_B X, Tr_B Y] = [[0, 4], [−4, 0]]</code>, which equals <code>+4i·σy</code>. A
      factorization that is legal on the whole system can therefore give a marginal that is not.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two true statements sit beside it. The boundary version holds: if the boundary operator
      commutes with M, its kept-side part commutes with <code>Tr_B M</code>. And
      <code>Tr_B([Z⊗1, M]) = [Z, Tr_B M]</code> holds without a hypothesis. The second lets the
      crate turn a measured residual into a bound with a stated <code>√(d_B)</code> amplification.
      A commutator within tolerance is warrant for the conclusion at <code>√(d_B)</code> times
      that tolerance, and no warrant for the exact one, so the check returns the bound and a flag
      against the caller’s tolerance.
    </p>
    <p>
      The boundary theorem’s hypothesis is exact equality over a commutative ring, and a
      floating-point caller cannot discharge it directly. That gap is why the bound exists.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="tests">
    <h2>What do the tests cover?</h2>
    <p>
      {TESTS.count} tests pass under
      <code>cargo test -p deep_causality_quantum --all-features</code>: 772 unit and integration
      tests and one doc test. Each check has tests, including tests where it must reject and tests
      that read a report on a case with nothing to examine. Every example printed identical output
      when run twice on one machine. A grep for the test attribute finds a different number, because it counts
      attributes and not the tests the harness runs.
    </p>
    <p class="next-links">
      <a href={`${CRATE_URL}/tests`}>The test tree ↗</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="deferred">
    <h2>What is stated and not proved?</h2>
    <p>
      These {TESTS.deferred} targets are named in the theorem map and in
      <a href={LEAN_STATUS_URL}><code>LEAN_QUANTUM.md</code></a>. The Rust behaviour behind each
      has tests today. No Lean proof exists yet, and the page sets no schedule.
    </p>
    <dl class="deflist">
      {deferred.map((d) => (
        <div>
          <dt><code>{d.id}</code></dt>
          <dd>
            <p class="dstate">{d.statement}</p>
            <p class="dtoday">{d.today}</p>
          </dd>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>
    <p>
      The Markov check, the decomposability check, every code check and the numeric naturality
      residual therefore stand on tests. The
      <a href="/checks/">checks page</a> marks each one.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="papers">
    <h2>Which papers does the crate implement?</h2>
    <p>
      {TESTS.papers} PDFs are committed under <a href={PAPERS_URL}><code>papers/</code></a>. The
      first list names the papers a module cites. A committed file is not a claim, so the second
      list holds the paper that no module cites.
    </p>
    <ol class="papers">
      {cited.map((p) => (
        <li>
          <p class="ptitle">{p.title}</p>
          <p class="pmeta">{p.authors}. {p.ref}, {p.year}.</p>
          {p.usedFor && <p class="pused">{p.usedFor}</p>}
          <p class="pcite">Cited from <code>{p.citedFrom.slice(0, 4).join(', ')}</code>{p.citedFrom.length > 4 ? ` and ${p.citedFrom.length - 4} more` : ''}.</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>

    <h3>Committed and not cited</h3>
    <ol class="papers">
      {uncited.map((p) => (
        <li>
          <p class="ptitle">{p.title}</p>
          <p class="pmeta">{p.authors}. {p.ref}, {p.year}.</p>
          <p class="pcite">No module in <code>src/</code>, the tests, the README or the changelog cites it.</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>

    <h3>Cited from the source, not committed</h3>
    <ul class="papers">
      {citedElsewhere.map((c) => (
        <li>
          <p class="pmeta">{c.work}</p>
          <p class="pcite">Cited from <code>{c.citedFrom}</code>.</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>
</PageShell>

<style>
  .tid { color: var(--fg-2); background: none; padding: 0; font-size: var(--t-eyebrow); }
  .table-scroll table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; }
  .table-scroll th, .table-scroll td { text-align: left; vertical-align: top; padding: var(--space-3); border-top: 1px solid var(--line-1); }
  .table-scroll thead th {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--t-eyebrow); letter-spacing: var(--ls-eyebrow);
    text-transform: uppercase; font-weight: 400; color: var(--fg-2); border-top: none;
  }
  .table-scroll td code { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  @media (min-width: 720px) {
    .table-scroll table { table-layout: fixed; }
    .table-scroll th:nth-child(1) { width: 29%; }
    .table-scroll th:nth-child(2) { width: 31%; }
    .table-scroll th:nth-child(3) { width: 40%; }
  }
  .item { background: none; padding: 0; color: var(--fg-0); font-size: var(--t-mono-sm); }
  .fpath { color: var(--fg-2); font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--t-eyebrow); overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  .dstate { margin: 0 0 var(--space-1); color: var(--fg-0); }
  .dtoday { margin: 0; color: var(--fg-1); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); }

  .papers { list-style: none; margin: 0 0 var(--space-5); padding: 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--line-1); max-width: var(--measure); }
  .papers li { padding: var(--space-4) 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-1); }
  .papers p { margin: 0 0 var(--space-1); }
  .ptitle { color: var(--fg-0); font-weight: var(--fw-heading); }
  .pmeta { color: var(--fg-1); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); }
  .pused { color: var(--fg-1); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); }
  .pcite { color: var(--fg-2); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); }
  .pcite code { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
</style>
